13:11:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It isn’t necessarily taboo. Casual generalizations about professions can be useful shorthand when people understand they describe tendencies, not rules. The difference is mostly in how the label lands: - **“Detail-oriented”** describes a work-related skill. - **“Argumentative”** can be negative, but at least points to a recognizable part of legal work. - **“Nerdy”** is broad and socially loaded. Depending on context, it can mean curious and enthusiastic, or awkward, unfashionable, obsessive, and undesirable. So saying, “Math departments often attract people who enjoy chess, science fiction, games, or highly focused intellectual hobbies” is a reasonable observation about a subculture. Saying, “Your cousin is a nerd because she’s a mathematician,” or expecting her to act a certain way, is where it becomes limiting. She also need not treat “nerdy” as an insult if it genuinely fits and she likes the term. The key is that she gets to decide whether that identity describes her—not that other people assign it from her job title.
